  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/support/stored_graph.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]` is a fenced commentary citation on an explicit function with a body, which is a permitted form. Its commentary gives a substantive reason for the unexecuted body, not just difficulty.\n\n- **Why the function exists:** `Domain` requires `outgoing_edges`, so a storage-only fixture has to define it. `crates/trust-but-verify/src/domain.rs#trait[Domain]/fn[outgoing_edges]` declares it.\n- **What the body does:** it deliberately panics with \"storage operations must not discover graph edges\". That matches the commentary's description of a guard that rejects unwanted graph access.\n- **Agreement with the current source:** the sibling `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` implementations in `StoredGraph/Domain` delegate through `node_path` only. The test `consumer_delegates_evidence_storage_using_only_the_requested_nodes_path` in `tests/storage.rs` uses `StoredGraph`, which fits the claim that the delegation test uses only `node_path`.\n- **Traversal fixtures:** other `Domain` fixtures, such as `Memory` in `src/agent/tests.rs` and `src/graph/validation_tests.rs`, implement real `outgoing_edges` and exercise edge discovery. This supports the claim that traversal fixtures cover it separately.\n\nThe item has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to the cited built-in, so the citation is not redundant. I found no gap.",
